\title{Conservation, Boundary Topology, and Symmetry in Jigsaw Assembly}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-10}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study the combinatorial structure of jigsaw puzzles through the algebra of
their edges. Each piece is modelled by the four-tuple of shapes on its top,
right, bottom, and left edges, where every edge is \emph{flat}, a \emph{tab}, or a
\emph{blank}, and two edges \emph{interlock} precisely when they are exchanged by
a single complementation involution swapping tab and blank while fixing flat. Two
threads of results follow. First, the decision problem of whether a given multiset
of pieces admits a valid assembly is NP-complete: Boolean satisfiability reduces
to puzzle assembly by encoding a truth value as an edge shape (true as a tab,
false as a blank), so that the local matching rule enforces logical consistency.
Second, we introduce a signed edge potential ($+1$ for a tab, $-1$ for a blank,
$0$ for a flat edge) and prove a \emph{conservation law}: every validly assembled
row or rectangle has total potential zero, so the number of exposed tabs equals
the number of exposed blanks. The proof reduces to a one-dimensional telescoping
cancellation and behaves like a discrete divergence theorem, with all interior
interfaces cancelling and a flat boundary carrying no charge. We accompany the
conservation law with a boundary-topology reading (corner pieces are doubly flat;
a handshake identity relates pieces, interior seams, and border edges) and a
determination of the symmetry group of the interlocking relation: a relabelling of
edge shapes preserves matching if and only if it commutes with complementation,
and there are exactly two such relabellings, so the automorphism group is
$\mathbb{Z}/2$.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

A jigsaw puzzle is a system of local constraints whose global solution encodes a
surprising amount of mathematics. We make this precise along two axes. On the
\emph{computational} axis, deciding whether a bag of pieces can be assembled into
a valid rectangle is NP-complete, placing jigsaw assembly alongside Boolean
satisfiability: easy to verify, apparently hard to solve. On the \emph{structural}
axis --- our main contribution --- we show that beneath the hardness there is exact
rigidity: every valid assembly obeys a conservation law. We isolate the algebraic
fact responsible (complementation negates a signed edge potential), prove one
telescoping lemma, and derive the conservation law in one and two dimensions, a
boundary-topology description, and the automorphism group of the matching
relation. The recurring character is a single order-two involution.

\section{The edge algebra}

\begin{definition}[Edges]
The \emph{edge alphabet} is the three-element set
$\Edges = \{\Eflat,\ \tab,\ \blank\}$. A flat edge is a straight border edge; a tab
protrudes outward; a blank recedes inward.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Complementation]
The \emph{complementation} map $\comp : \Edges \to \Edges$ is
$\comp(\Eflat) = \Eflat$, $\comp(\tab) = \blank$, $\comp(\blank) = \tab$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Involution]\label{lem:inv}
$\comp \circ \comp = \mathrm{id}_{\Edges}$; its set of fixed points is exactly
$\{\Eflat\}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Immediate by checking the three cases.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Fitting]
Two edges $a, b \in \Edges$ \emph{interlock}, written $a \fits b$, when
$b = \comp(a)$. A tab fits a blank, a blank fits a tab, and a flat fits nothing at
an interior interface.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Pieces]
A \emph{piece} is a tuple
$p = (p.\text{top}, p.\text{right}, p.\text{bottom}, p.\text{left}) \in \Edges^4$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Truth encoding]
The \emph{encoding} $\enc : \{\bot, \top\} \to \Edges$ sends $\top \mapsto \tab$
and $\bot \mapsto \blank$: a truth value is stored as the sign of an interlocking
edge.
\end{definition}

\section{Computational hardness}

\begin{theorem}[NP-completeness of assembly]
The decision problem ``given a finite multiset of pieces and target dimensions
$r \times c$, is there a valid assembly?'' is NP-complete.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Membership in NP is clear: a claimed placement is a polynomial-size certificate
whose validity (all interior interfaces complementary, all border edges flat) is
checkable in polynomial time. For hardness we reduce from Boolean satisfiability.
Given a formula $\varphi$ over $x_1,\dots,x_m$, build pieces arranged into
\emph{channels}, one per variable, whose interlocking edges must carry a
consistent value. Since neighbouring edges must be complementary and a truth value
is $\enc(b)$ with $\enc(\top)=\tab$, $\enc(\bot)=\blank$, a channel assembles in
exactly two ways, corresponding to $x_k=\top$ or $x_k=\bot$. Clause gadgets connect
channels so that a clause's gadget admits a valid local assembly iff at least one
literal is satisfied. The board assembles iff some assignment satisfies every
clause, i.e. iff $\varphi$ is satisfiable; the construction is polynomial in
$|\varphi|$.
\end{proof}

\section{The signed potential and its two defining properties}

\begin{definition}[Edge potential]
The \emph{potential} $\wt : \Edges \to \mathbb{Z}$ is
$\wt(\Eflat)=0$, $\wt(\tab)=+1$, $\wt(\blank)=-1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Complementation negates potential]\label{lem:negate}
For every edge $e$, $\ \wt(\comp(e)) = -\,\wt(e)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Check the three cases directly.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Potential detects the boundary]\label{lem:detect}
$\wt(e) = 0$ if and only if $e = \Eflat$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Encoding sign]
$\wt(\enc(b)) = +1$ if $b=\top$ and $-1$ if $b=\bot$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Potential as indicator]\label{lem:indicator}
For every edge $e$, $\ \wt(e) = \ind[e=\tab] - \ind[e=\blank]$. Consequently,
summed over any collection of edges, total potential equals (number of tabs) minus
(number of blanks).
\end{lemma}

\section{The telescoping conservation lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Telescoping cancellation]\label{lem:strip}
Let $r, \ell : \mathbb{N} \to \Edges$ be sequences of right and left edges along a
strip of $n$ consecutive interfaces. Suppose: (1) if $n>0$ then $\ell(0)=\Eflat$;
(2) if $n>0$ then $r(n-1)=\Eflat$; (3) for all $i$ with $i+1<n$,
$\ell(i+1)=\comp(r(i))$. Then
\[
\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} \wt(r(i)) + \sum_{i=0}^{n-1} \wt(\ell(i)) = 0.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The case $n=0$ is the empty sum. For $n=m+1$, split off the last right term and
the first left term. By (1)--(2), $\wt(\ell(0))=0$ and $\wt(r(m))=0$. By (3) and
Lemma~\ref{lem:negate},
$\sum_{i=0}^{m-1}\wt(\ell(i+1)) = -\sum_{i=0}^{m-1}\wt(r(i))$. Adding, the two
copies of $\sum_{i=0}^{m-1}\wt(r(i))$ cancel.
\end{proof}

\section{Rows: one-dimensional conservation}

\begin{definition}[Valid row]
A \emph{row} of length $n$ is pieces $p_0,\dots,p_{n-1}$ laid left to right. It is
\emph{valid} when every $p_i$ has flat top and bottom, $p_0.\text{left}$ and
$p_{n-1}.\text{right}$ are flat, and $p_{i+1}.\text{left}=\comp(p_i.\text{right})$
for all interior $i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Row conservation]
Every valid row has total potential zero.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Flat tops and bottoms contribute $0$, so the potential equals
$\sum_i(\wt(p_i.\text{right})+\wt(p_i.\text{left}))$. Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:strip}
with $r(i)=p_i.\text{right}$, $\ell(i)=p_i.\text{left}$; the hypotheses are exactly
validity.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Tab--blank balance, 1D]
In every valid row, the number of exposed tabs equals the number of exposed blanks.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:indicator}, potential $=$ tabs $-$ blanks, which is zero.
\end{proof}

\section{Rectangles: two-dimensional conservation}

\begin{definition}[Valid grid]
A \emph{grid} $R\times C$ is pieces $p_{i,j}$. It is \emph{valid} when its four
borders are flat and interior neighbours interlock:
$p_{i,j+1}.\text{left}=\comp(p_{i,j}.\text{right})$ and
$p_{i+1,j}.\text{top}=\comp(p_{i,j}.\text{bottom})$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Slice conservation]\label{thm:slice}
In a valid grid, each row has zero horizontal potential and each column has zero
vertical potential.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each is a direct application of Lemma~\ref{lem:strip} --- along the row using the
flat left/right borders and the horizontal interface rule, along the column using
the flat top/bottom borders and the vertical rule.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Grid conservation]
Every valid grid has total potential zero:
\[
\sum_{i=0}^{R-1}\sum_{j=0}^{C-1}\big(\wt(p_{i,j}.\text{top})+\wt(p_{i,j}.\text{right})
+\wt(p_{i,j}.\text{bottom})+\wt(p_{i,j}.\text{left})\big)=0.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Regroup as (all right/left potentials) $+$ (all top/bottom potentials). The first
is $\sum_i$ of the horizontal row potentials, each zero by
Theorem~\ref{thm:slice}; the second is $\sum_j$ of the vertical column potentials
after exchanging summation order, each zero by Theorem~\ref{thm:slice}.
\end{proof}

By Lemma~\ref{lem:indicator}, every valid rectangular assembly has as many exposed
tabs as blanks. The two-dimensional statement follows by slicing into
one-dimensional strips --- the combinatorial shadow of a divergence theorem.

\section{Boundary topology}

\begin{theorem}[Corner pieces are doubly flat]
In a non-empty valid grid, the top-left corner piece satisfies
$p_{0,0}.\text{top}=\Eflat$ and $p_{0,0}.\text{left}=\Eflat$; symmetrically for the
other corners.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Directly from validity: the top edge lies on the flat top border and the left edge
on the flat left border.
\end{proof}

By Lemma~\ref{lem:detect}, the boundary is exactly the fixed-point set of
complementation.

\begin{theorem}[Handshake identity]
In an $(r+1)\times(c+1)$ grid,
\[
2\big((r+1)c + r(c+1)\big) + 2\big((r+1)+(c+1)\big) = 4\,(r+1)(c+1).
\]
Equivalently, twice the interior interfaces plus the border edges equals four times
the pieces.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each of the $(r+1)(c+1)$ pieces has four edges. Every interior interface is shared
by two pieces (there are $(r+1)c$ vertical and $r(c+1)$ horizontal seams); every
border edge belongs to one piece (there are $2((r+1)+(c+1))$). Counting edge-slots
both ways gives the identity.
\end{proof}

\section{The symmetry group of interlocking}

\begin{definition}[Commuting with complementation]
A permutation $\sigma$ of $\Edges$ \emph{commutes with complementation} when
$\sigma(\comp(e))=\comp(\sigma(e))$ for every $e$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Symmetry characterization]
A permutation $\sigma$ preserves the interlocking relation ---
$a\fits b \iff \sigma(a)\fits\sigma(b)$ for all $a,b$ --- if and only if it commutes
with complementation.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) Since $e\fits\comp(e)$, preservation gives
$\sigma(\comp(e))=\comp(\sigma(e))$. ($\Leftarrow$) If $\sigma\comp=\comp\sigma$
then $b=\comp(a)$ implies $\sigma(b)=\comp(\sigma(a))$; conversely
$\sigma(b)=\comp(\sigma(a))=\sigma(\comp(a))$ and injectivity give $b=\comp(a)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Order of the symmetry group]
Exactly two permutations of $\Edges$ commute with complementation: the identity and
the $\tab\leftrightarrow\blank$ swap. Hence the automorphism group of interlocking
is $\mathbb{Z}/2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Such a $\sigma$ fixes the fixed-point set $\{\Eflat\}$ of $\comp$, so
$\sigma(\Eflat)=\Eflat$ and $\sigma$ permutes $\{\tab,\blank\}$. Both permutations of
this pair commute with $\comp$; no other permutation of $\Edges$ fixes flat.
\end{proof}

Thus one involution negates the potential (powering conservation), fixes exactly
the flat edges (describing the boundary), and generates the order-two symmetry
group of matching.

\section{Worked example}

Consider the two-piece row with $p_0=(\Eflat,\tab,\Eflat,\Eflat)$ and
$p_1=(\Eflat,\Eflat,\Eflat,\blank)$. The interior interface pairs
$p_0.\text{right}=\tab$ with $p_1.\text{left}=\blank=\comp(\tab)$; all other edges
are flat. The row is valid, exposes one tab and one blank, and has total potential
$(+1)+(-1)=0$.

\section{Applications and discussion}

The conservation law is a global invariant computable in linear time: any candidate
assembly whose tab count differs from its blank count is provably invalid --- a
checksum for puzzles. The proof structure (interior cancellation, boundary charge)
is precisely that of a discrete divergence theorem, with the signed potential as
flux, complementation as orientation reversal across a shared face, and the flat
border as a no-flux boundary condition. Notably, a problem NP-complete to
\emph{solve} nonetheless obeys an exact, efficiently checkable law satisfied by
\emph{every} solution: hardness lives in \emph{finding} an assembly, while the
conservation law constrains the \emph{space} of assemblies.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Simply connected regions]
The tab--blank balance holds for every assembly whose footprint is a simply
connected region of the square lattice with an all-flat outer boundary. The signed
potential behaves like a discrete divergence: complementation negates it, shared
interior edges cancel, and only the boundary carries net charge; a flat boundary
carries none. Extending the slicing argument to staircase and L-shaped footprints
would connect puzzle assembly to discrete exterior calculus.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Genus obstructions]
On a closed surface of genus $g$, the balance is corrected by a term determined
solely by $g$: exact on the torus, with a fixed unremovable defect on higher-genus
surfaces. The boundary term is a topological invariant of the assembly surface; the
unpaired remainder matches an Euler-characteristic count.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Richer alphabets]
If the edge alphabet is enlarged to $2k+1$ shapes (one self-complementary border
shape and $k$ complementary tab/blank pairs), the automorphism group of interlocking
is the hyperoctahedral group of order $2^k\cdot k!$, with centre the single
order-two ``flip every interlock'' symmetry. Preserving the matching relation is
equivalent to commuting with complementation, and for $k$ independent pairs this
centraliser is the group of signed permutations of the pairs.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}

From the single fact that complementation is an involution negating a signed edge
potential, we obtained a conservation law forcing exposed tabs and blanks to
balance in every valid row and rectangle, a boundary-topology reading with
doubly-flat corners and a handshake identity, and the determination of the matching
relation's automorphism group as $\mathbb{Z}/2$. Alongside the classical
NP-completeness of assembly, the jigsaw puzzle emerges as a compact laboratory where
computational hardness, conservation, topology, and symmetry meet.

